{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_000000000000000000000001","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_000000000000000000000001","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/work/essay.md","content":"# A Short History of Version Control\n\n## Before Version Control\n\nSoftware began as a small craft. Early programs lived on punched cards and paper tape, and a programmer's history was a physical stack. If you wanted to keep an old version, you kept the old deck. If you wanted to change a program, you pulled cards out, punched new ones, and put them back. The record of what changed lived in the programmer's memory and in margin notes.\n\nAs programs grew, this stopped working. By the 1960s, large projects such as IBM's OS/360 involved hundreds of engineers working on millions of lines of code. Teams needed answers to simple questions. Who changed this file? Why did it change? Which version did we ship to the customer? Which change broke the build? Without tools, people answered these questions with naming conventions and discipline. Files were copied into directories named final, final2 and really_final. Many developers still recognise this habit today, because it never fully went away.\n\nTwo problems drove the first tools. The first was history: a developer needed to recover an earlier state of a file after a mistake. The second was coordination: two people editing the same file at the same time would overwrite each other's work. Every version control system since has been an attempt to solve these two problems with less pain than the system before it.\n\nThe first answers came from the same place as the problem, the large mainframe shops. They stored files on shared machines, so the machine itself could keep track of changes. The idea was simple. Instead of saving each full copy of a file, store the first version and then store only the differences between versions. This saved disk space, which was expensive, and it gave a reader a trail of edits. Delta storage became the foundation of almost everything that followed.\n\nThe tools of this era were small and local. They had no network, no branches in the modern sense, and no idea of a project beyond a single file. But they set the vocabulary we still use: revision, check in, check out, and diff.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":24582,"cache_read_input_tokens":11391,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":24582},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001","uuid":"00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000009","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:06:47.573Z","request_id":"req_000000000000000000000001","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_000000000000000000000001":{"file_path":"/work/essay.md","content":"# A Short History of Version Control\n\n## Before Version Control\n\nSoftware began as a small craft. Early programs lived on punched cards and paper tape, and a programmer's history was a physical stack. If you wanted to keep an old version, you kept the old deck. If you wanted to change a program, you pulled cards out, punched new ones, and put them back. The record of what changed lived in the programmer's memory and in margin notes.\n\nAs programs grew, this stopped working. By the 1960s, large projects such as IBM's OS/360 involved hundreds of engineers working on millions of lines of code. Teams needed answers to simple questions. Who changed this file? Why did it change? Which version did we ship to the customer? Which change broke the build? Without tools, people answered these questions with naming conventions and discipline. Files were copied into directories named final, final2