ine itself could keep track of changes. The idea was simple. Instead of saving each full copy of a file, store the first version and then store only the differences between versions. This saved disk space, which was expensive, and it gave a reader a trail of edits. Delta storage became the foundation of almost everything that followed.\n\nThe tools of this era were small and local. They had no network, no branches in the modern sense, and no idea of a project beyond a single file. But they set the vocabulary we still use: revision, check in, check out, and diff.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_000000000000000000000001","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_000000000000000000000002","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_000000000000000000000002","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat >> essay.md <<'EOF'\n\n## SCCS and RCS: Tracking Single Files\n\nThe first widely known version control tool was SCCS, the Source Code Control System. Marc Rochkind wrote it at Bell Labs in 1972, first for IBM System/370 and later for Unix. SCCS stored every file as a single \"weave\" of all its revisions. A reader could rebuild any revision by choosing which parts of the weave to include. It also introduced locking: when you checked out a file to edit it, nobody else could edit it until you checked it back in.\n\nLocking solved the overwrite problem in the most direct way possible. It also made teams wait. If a colleague held the lock on a core file and went to lunch, or on holiday, your work stopped. This tension between safety and speed shaped the next thirty years of design.\n\nIn 1982, Walter Tichy released RCS, the Revision Control System, at Purdue University. RCS was smaller and faster than SCCS. It stored the newest revision in full and older revisions as reverse deltas, so the most common operation, getting the latest version, was also the quickest. RCS kept its data in files ending in \",v\" beside the working files, and it remained a popular tool for system administrators who wanted to track a few configuration files. Some still use it for that.\n\nBoth tools shared a limit. They worked on one file at a time. A real project is a set of files that must change together: a header, its implementation, and the tests that cover them. SCCS and RCS could tell you the history of each file, but they could not tell you that three files were changed as one logical step. Developers worked around this by tagging a set of file revisions with a shared symbolic name, such as a release label. It worked, but it was fragile.\n\nThey also had no real answer for people on different machines. Everyone had to share one file system, or someone had to copy files by hand. As Unix networks spread through universities and companies in the 1980s, the need for a tool that understood both whole projects and networks became clear.\nEOF"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1203,"cache_read_input_tokens":35973,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1203},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001","uuid":"00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:06:54.977Z","request_id":"req_000000000000000000000002","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_000000000000000000000002":{"command":"cd /work && cat >> essay.md <<'EOF'\n\n## SCCS and RCS: Tracking Single Files\n\nThe first widely known version control tool was SCCS, the Source Code Control System. Marc Rochkind wrote it at Bell Labs in 1972, first for IBM System/370 and later for Unix. SCCS stored every file as a single \"weave\" of all its revisions. A reader could rebuild any revision by choosing which parts of the weave to include. It also introduced locking: when you checked out a file to edit it, nobody else could edit it until you checked it back in.\n\nLocking solved the overwrite problem in the most direct way possible. It also made teams wait. If a colleague held the lock on a core file and went to lunch, or on holiday, your work stopped. This tension between safety and speed shaped the next thirty years of design.\n\nIn 1982, Walter Tichy released RCS, the Revision Control System, at Purdue University. RCS was smaller and faster than SCCS. It stored the newest revision in full and older revisions as